A swapchain rebuild must not destroy an acquire semaphore that still has a
pending signal, and must not destroy images a submitted frame is still using.

Found by lane VR-3b (2026-09-17), hunting the owner's WiVRn crash: the first
frame of a VR session died with

    OgreEngine::renderOneFrame -> OgreView::syncReflectListener
      -> ~ReflectPassListener -> RenderSystem::flushCommands
      -> VulkanQueue::commitAndNextCommandBuffer -> vkQueueSubmit
      -> VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST

and NO `NVRM: Xid` in the kernel log at that time — i.e. the GPU never hung; the
driver REJECTED the submission. The two things that happen immediately before it
are the two swapchain rebuilds a VR session start triggers (the render driver
turns vsync off for the session, EngineRenderDriver::setVrSessionActive).

THE MECHANISM, measured under VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation on the rig (Monado's
simulated HMD, twelve VR entry/exit cycles):

    Validation Error: [ VUID-vkAcquireNextImageKHR-semaphore-01779 ]
    vkAcquireNextImageKHR(): Semaphore must not have any pending operations.

twice per VR entry, and never in a run without one. The sequence:

  * a frame ends: commitAndNextCommandBuffer(EndFrameAndSwap) presents and then
    calls acquireNextSwapchain(), which takes a semaphore from the VaoManager's
    pool and hands it to vkAcquireNextImageKHR. That semaphore now has a SIGNAL
    operation pending, and only a queue submit that WAITS on it consumes that
    signal (vkDeviceWaitIdle does not, and there is no other consumer).
  * before the next frame renders, the vsync change destroys the swapchain.
    `destroySwapchain` called `VulkanVaoManager::notifySemaphoreUnused`, which is
    a bare `vkDestroySemaphore` — no fence, no deferral, and with the acquire's
    signal still unconsumed.
  * the handle is freed, the driver hands the SAME handle back from the next
    vkCreateSemaphore, and the recreated swapchain's acquire is given a semaphore
    the validation layer still knows has pending operations. From there the next
    vkQueueSubmit is a submit whose wait list, fence state and semaphore
    ownership the driver no longer agrees with — VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST, with no
    GPU fault.

THE FIX, in the pin's own vocabulary and on the pin's own precedent:

  1. `destroySwapchain` retires the semaphore instead of destroying it. From
     SwapchainAcquired (acquired, never rendered into) the acquire's signal is
     consumed by submitting a wait for it — one empty command buffer, which
     commitAndNextCommandBuffer produces anyway — and from SwapchainUsedInRendering
     the wait the frame already recorded is submitted. Either way the semaphore
     goes back through `notifyWaitSemaphoreSubmitted`, the SAME deferred route
     `swapBuffers()` uses for the ordinary case (recycled after
     mDynamicBufferMultiplier frames). The final teardown and a lost device keep
     the old immediate destroy — no submit is possible there — and the re-entrant
     call (acquireNextSwapchain -> windowMovedOrResized -> destroySwapchain)
     arrives in SwapchainReleased, so it never submits from inside a submit.
  2. `setVSync` and `setWantsToDownload` stall before rebuilding, which is what
     the sibling rebuild site already does (VulkanXcbWindow::windowMovedOrResized
     calls stallIgnoringDeviceLost before its own destroySwapchain). Without it
     the presentable images of the swapchain being destroyed can still be in
     flight in a submitted frame (VUID-vkDestroySwapchainKHR-swapchain-01282).
     A vsync change already costs a full swapchain rebuild; it now costs a device
     idle with it, on an operation that happens on a pacing change and at a VR
     session's start and end — never per frame.

MEASURED: twelve VR entry/exit cycles under the validation layer, before and
after, on one binary each: 20 reports of VUID-vkAcquireNextImageKHR-semaphore-01779
before (the layer's duplicate limit; two per cycle), ZERO after. The three
unrelated findings that run carries (the VCT light-injection pair
VUID-vkCmdDispatch-None-08114 / VUID-VkWriteDescriptorSet-descriptorType-00319,
and VUID-vkCmdDraw-None-09600) are unchanged and are present without VR too.

HONEST SCOPE: this is not proof that the owner's DEVICE_LOST was this and only
this — that crash has not been reproduced on the rig (Monado's simulated runtime
survives the same sequence). It is a real, spec-level misuse by the pin, on the
exact two operations that immediately precede the crash, of exactly the class a
driver answers with a synchronous VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST and no Xid.

SOURCE patch: every tree re-runs irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh.
It shares RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanWindow.cpp with patches 0008 and
0013, so expect it on the reverse-check's overlap list.
AMENDED AT MERGE (the lead, after the Fable second read, 2026-09-17): (a) the
SUBOPTIMAL / mSuboptimal acquire path also rebuilds the swapchain, and there the
acquire DID return an image (the semaphore will signal) while the status was
still Released, so destroySwapchain took the bare-destroy branch -- the very
defect this patch fixes, reachable on any real window-manager resize (Xvfb never
reports SUBOPTIMAL, which is why the lane's twelve cycles did not see it). The
rebuild path now marks the status Acquired when the acquire returned an image;
OUT_OF_DATE acquired nothing and stays Released. (b) The retirement runs BEFORE
notifySwapchainDestroyed releases the framebuffers. (c) The Acquired branch
stalls after its submit, so the swapchain that owns the pending signal is not
destroyed while the wait that consumes it is still queued.

--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanWindow.cpp
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanWindow.cpp
@@ -607,14 +607,68 @@
     void VulkanWindowSwapChainBased::destroySwapchain( bool finalDestruction )
     {
         OGRE_ANDROID_SURFACE_LOCK;
-        mDevice->mRenderSystem->notifySwapchainDestroyed( this );
 
         if( mSwapchainSemaphore )
         {
-            mDevice->mVaoManager->notifySemaphoreUnused( mSwapchainSemaphore );
+            // JAHSHAKA PATCH 0073: a swapchain's acquire semaphore may not be
+            // DESTROYED while it still carries an unconsumed signal (or a wait
+            // that has been recorded but not yet submitted). vkAcquireNextImageKHR
+            // signals this semaphore; only a queue submit that WAITS on it
+            // consumes that signal, and vkDeviceWaitIdle does not. Destroying it
+            // here, as this function used to unconditionally do, leaves the
+            // signal pending on a freed handle: the driver hands the same handle
+            // back from the next vkCreateSemaphore, the next acquire is given a
+            // semaphore with pending operations (VUID-vkAcquireNextImageKHR-
+            // semaphore-01779, measured twice per VR session start), and the next
+            // vkQueueSubmit can be rejected outright with VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST —
+            // with no GPU fault at all, which is what made it look like a driver
+            // flake. So: submit the wait, then RETIRE the semaphore through the
+            // manager's deferred path (notifyWaitSemaphoreSubmitted), which is
+            // the same route swapBuffers() uses for the ordinary case.
+            //
+            // Only from a state that can hold a pending operation, and never on
+            // the final teardown or a lost device, where no submit is possible —
+            // those keep the old immediate destroy. The re-entrant call
+            // (acquireNextSwapchain -> windowMovedOrResized -> destroySwapchain)
+            // arrives in SwapchainAcquired when the acquire returned an image
+            // (SUBOPTIMAL, or SUCCESS with mSuboptimal set from the last present)
+            // and in SwapchainReleased when it did not (OUT_OF_DATE) -- see
+            // acquireNextSwapchain. A nested FlushOnly submit from inside the
+            // queue's own commit tail is legal there: the new command buffer is
+            // already open and the pending list cleared, and the xcb resize path
+            // already stalls from that very spot.
+            //
+            // This block runs BEFORE notifySwapchainDestroyed releases the
+            // framebuffers, so a submitted wait never references a released one;
+            // and the Acquired branch STALLS after its submit so the swapchain
+            // that owns the pending signal is not destroyed while the wait that
+            // consumes it is still queued (the WSI spec's grey area; a driver that
+            // rejects submissions over semaphore state is what this patch is for).
+            // The stall is a device idle on an operation that happens on a pacing
+            // change and at a VR session's start and end -- never per frame.
+            const bool canSubmit = !finalDestruction && !mDevice->isDeviceLost();
+            if( canSubmit && ( mSwapchainStatus == SwapchainAcquired ||
+                               mSwapchainStatus == SwapchainUsedInRendering ) )
+            {
+                if( mSwapchainStatus == SwapchainAcquired )
+                {
+                    // Acquired but never rendered into: nothing waits on the
+                    // acquire's signal yet, so this batch is what consumes it.
+                    mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.addWindowToWaitFor( mSwapchainSemaphore );
+                }
+                mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.commitAndNextCommandBuffer( SubmissionType::FlushOnly );
+                mDevice->stallIgnoringDeviceLost();
+                mDevice->mVaoManager->notifyWaitSemaphoreSubmitted( mSwapchainSemaphore );
+            }
+            else
+            {
+                mDevice->mVaoManager->notifySemaphoreUnused( mSwapchainSemaphore );
+            }
             mSwapchainSemaphore = 0;
         }
 
+        mDevice->mRenderSystem->notifySwapchainDestroyed( this );
+
         if( mSwapchain )
         {
             vkDestroySwapchainKHR( mDevice->mDevice, mSwapchain, 0 );
@@ -684,6 +738,13 @@
                   ( result == VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR || result == VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR ) ) &&
                 !mRebuildingSwapchain )
             {
+                // JAHSHAKA PATCH 0073: if the acquire RETURNED AN IMAGE (SUCCESS
+                // with mSuboptimal from the last present, or SUBOPTIMAL), the
+                // semaphore WILL be signalled, and the rebuild's destroySwapchain
+                // must retire it through a submitted wait, not destroy it. Say so
+                // in the status; OUT_OF_DATE acquired nothing and stays Released.
+                if( result == VK_SUCCESS || result == VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR )
+                    mSwapchainStatus = SwapchainAcquired;
                 mRebuildingSwapchain = true;
                 windowMovedOrResized();
                 mRebuildingSwapchain = false;
@@ -760,6 +821,13 @@
         // Don't do it right now because we need to recreate the surface as well.
         OGRE_ANDROID_SURFACE_PREVENT_USE;
 
+        // JAHSHAKA PATCH 0073: the presentable images of the swapchain about to
+        // be destroyed may still be in flight in a submitted frame
+        // (VUID-vkDestroySwapchainKHR-swapchain-01282). Every other rebuild site
+        // knows this — VulkanXcbWindow::windowMovedOrResized stalls before its
+        // own destroySwapchain() — and a VSync change is the same rebuild.
+        mDevice->stallIgnoringDeviceLost();
+
         destroySwapchain();
         createSwapchain();
     }
@@ -775,6 +843,10 @@
         // Don't do it right now because we need to recreate the surface as well.
         OGRE_ANDROID_SURFACE_PREVENT_USE;
 
+        // JAHSHAKA PATCH 0073, as in setVSync above: a rebuild waits for the
+        // frames that are still using the old swapchain's images.
+        mDevice->stallIgnoringDeviceLost();
+
         destroySwapchain();
         createSwapchain();
     }
